<title>POLAND: Poland unlikely to restore grain duties early - govt.</title>
<headline>Poland unlikely to restore grain duties early - govt.</headline>
<dateline>WARSAW 1997-01-20</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Polish government is unlikely to restore duties on grains imports ahead of schedule, as demanded by the agriculture minister, the government spokeswoman said on Monday.</p>
<p>The duties are suspended until July 1997.</p>
<p>&quot;The decision to maintain duty-free grains imports until June 30 is right because...prices of bread and flour products are growing,&quot; Aleksandra Jakubowska told a news briefing.</p>
<p>Agriculture Minister Roman Jagielinski has said he will ask the government to end the duty-free grains imports as soon as possible because the grains market has stabilised following large imports and because the outlook for grains output promised strong supply.</p>
<p>Last year the government suspended the 20-percent duty on grains imports after prices began to climb following a bad harvest.</p>
<p>-- Marcin Grajewski +48 22 653 9700</p>
</text>
